Leg 1 is complete only when run state carries the release version and the
bump-MR merge SHA (`work release status` shows both). From that point the
standing `prep-release` → `main` MR is the release MR, and the gate holds
until a human merges it.

**The human merging the protected-`main` release MR on the canonical
GitLab repository IS the release approval.** There is no other approval
artifact — no comment, no label, no out-of-band acknowledgement counts.
NEVER merge the release MR yourself, never accept or approve it on the
human's behalf, and never treat any signal short of the actual merge as
the approval.

### Arming the gate

1. Record the transition BEFORE idling: `work state set --phase=gate`,
   then `work commit`. The gate is not armed until the pushed run state
   says so — a watcher idling on unpushed state is invisible to every
   other launch.
2. Arm the watch on the release MR:

   ```bash
   gitlab-review <project> <release-mr-iid> --host <hostname> --check-updates --watch --command "/followup"
   ```

   The `--check-updates --watch` primitive blocks on MR activity and
   fires the follow-up command when the MR changes; the merge is the
   activity this gate is waiting for.
3. Idle. Do no other work while the gate is open — the run advances only
   on the human's merge. A long idle will outlive the claim's stale
   threshold; that is expected and safe BECAUSE of the wake-time re-claim
   below, never a reason to hold the gate with a hand-rolled refresh loop.

On merge detection:

1. First, confirm the MR state is actually `merged`
   (`gitlab-review <project> <release-mr-iid> --host <hostname> --info` —
   a comment or pipeline event also wakes the watch).
2. Re-run `work release claim` BEFORE any other action. The idle was long;
   the claim this session took at start may have gone stale and been taken
   over by a scheduled relaunch — on the human's merge BOTH sessions wake,
   and without this re-arbitration they would race the tag creation. Exit 0
   means this session drives Leg 2; non-zero means another session holds
   the release — log it and end this session, exactly like the Phase 0.25
   refusal.
3. Proceed to Leg 2. Its FIRST action is recording the release MR's merge
   SHA into run state.

### Watch is best-effort; resume is the contract

The watcher session can die — container reaped, host rebooted — and
nothing is lost. Run state, not this session, is what holds the release
open:

- A merge that happens while nothing is watching is picked up at the
  next launch: relaunching this taskdef re-derives the leg from run
  state and continues (see "Resuming an in-progress release").
- The relaunch is scheduled declaratively, outside this repository —
  lmer ships no cron facility; the periodic launch of this taskdef while
  a release run is in-progress is documented in `docs/RELEASE-FLOW.md`.
  Manual relaunch works identically. Either way, a merged release MR
  never sits untagged longer than one schedule interval.
- If the installed `gitlab-review` does not support `--check-updates
  --watch`, do NOT substitute a hand-rolled watcher: verify state is
  pushed, log that the gate is held by state alone
  (`work event gate --note "watch unavailable; resume carries the gate"`),
  and end the session — the scheduled relaunch carries the gate.

### No memory across the gate

- NEVER hold the gate open with a polling loop that re-derives progress
  from session memory. Every wake, every transition, every resumption
  reads run state (`work release status`) before acting — the recorded
  version and SHAs are the only carriers across the gate, never
  variables this session happens to remember.
- Push state at EVERY transition: `work commit` before arming the watch,
  and again the moment merge detection records the merge SHA. Unpushed
  state does not exist to the next session.
